================================================================
Acknowledgements
================================================================

This driver was written from scratch for OPENSTEP 4.2. Its primary
specification is Intel's own manual, and two existing BSD-licensed
drivers were consulted while writing it. No source code from either was
copied into this work, and neither is redistributed here — the local
copies used during development live in ref/, which is excluded from the
repository and from the distribution tarball. They are acknowledged
because reading them saved real time and because several decisions in
this driver are cited to them in the source comments.

Intel, "PCI/PCI-X Family of Gigabit Ethernet Controllers Software
Developer's Manual" (317453, rev 4.0)

    The primary specification. Register offsets and layouts, the
    mandatory settling time after a global reset, the multicast hash,
    the CSA interrupt-mask ordering requirement for the 82547, and the
    statistics-register semantics all come from here.

FreeBSD, sys/dev/e1000 — Copyright (c) 2001-2020, Intel Corporation,
distributed under BSD-3-Clause

    The only readily available implementation that covers the 82547.
    Consulted for which parts of the family need the PHY reset issued
    before the MAC and which cannot accept a memory-space global reset
    (e1000_82541.c gates both on mac.type), for the set of receive
    descriptor error bits that mean a frame is bad
    (E1000_RXD_ERR_FRAME_ERR_MASK), and for the multicast hash. Its
    register headers were used as a second opinion: every offset in this
    driver was taken only after FreeBSD's e1000_regs.h and the Intel
    manual agreed on it.

Minix 3, minix/drivers/net/e1000 — distributed under BSD-3-Clause
(these files note that parts derive from DragonflyBSD/FreeBSD)

    A compact implementation, useful as a structural reference for how
    little is actually required to drive one of these parts. Its
    treatment of the transmit-queue-empty and transmit-descriptor-written
    interrupt causes as interchangeable is followed here. It does not
    support the 82547.

Also consulted, from the OPENSTEP installation itself: NeXT's SMC16 and
AMDPCSCSIDriver DriverKit examples, and the NextDev documentation for
the loadable-kernel-server load commands. These are NeXT's material,
were used as documentation of the platform's own conventions, and are
likewise not redistributed here.

On GPL sources. The project set out to treat BSD-licensed drivers as the
implementation reference and GPL ones (Linux, U-Boot) as background
reading only, never as something to copy from — the rule is recorded in
PLAN.md and ref/README.md. In the event no GPL e1000 source was even
available on the development machine, which carries Linux headers but no
driver sources. Nothing in this work derives from them.
